Rebuild the reviewed wrapper from the bundle with every correction at once.

  before: c36aa4343f8fda11ac1a9c445de70a1c659590e7690fe2019647da3e1593f63f
  after:  27bbeb907627095090623dd5cb97bd08b0740239c466976850f4bb4d23212fbf

This supersedes the three sequential patches that were applied to the running
wrapper during the incident -- 8088-predicate, dialplan-gate and journal-gate.
Those took the live file from c36aa434 to a5767237 one at a time, under time
pressure, while calls were down. This takes the *bundle* copy from the same
starting point to a wrapper that carries all three plus the new dialplan
transform digest, so the reviewed artifact and production stop disagreeing.

The three corrections are byte-identical to the live ones; only the transform
pin is new here, which is why the digest differs from a5767237.

## What went in

  1. verify_no_tcp_listener 8088 -> verify_no_tcp_listener_owned_by 8088 asterisk
     Asterisk's readiness proof demanded an empty 8088 while nginx's proof
     demanded nginx on it, in the same call, so the gate could never pass.
  2. the authorize-AGI check now asks for fanum-correlated-dial-gate
     It was searching fanum-observed-place-call, which does not contain that
     line -- dialplan show prints one context and does not expand a Gosub.
  3. the post-cutover journal gate ignores radiusclient-ng/radiusclient.conf
     This host logs that at error level on every Asterisk start, including the
     start the rollback itself performs.
  4. EXPECTED_DIALPLAN_TRANSFORM_SHA256 -> 2ef60581…
     The generator now derives the route mode instead of hard-coding "human";
     see dialplan-transform-route-mode.patch.

## Verification

The bundle's own suite passes in full after the reconciliation: 112 of 112,
including the static contract test that hashes the reviewed payload and the
transform test that pinned the old hard-coded route mode. The coordinator in
the bundle now hashes to 9ac291ac…, byte-for-byte the file production is
already running -- the strongest available evidence that the bundle and the
live host are the same code rather than merely similar.

## Still required before staging

The staging workflow assembles the payload from the bundle on the operator's
own machine, not from the copy on the production host that was reconciled here.
Those two must be made identical first, or staging will refuse the payload on a
digest mismatch. That is the one remaining step, and it needs that machine.
